Register GDAL's drivers in every extension that looks a driver name up, so
rasterio works on iOS where each extension links its own copy of GDAL.

`flet-libgdal` ships only `libgdal.a` on iOS — no shared library, and no
`flet-lib*` on this index ships one. Each rasterio extension therefore links
its own complete copy of GDAL, and each copy has its own driver registry.
`rasterio.Env()` lives in `_env` and calls `GDALAllRegister()` there; but
`rasterio.open()` resolves the driver name in `_base` (`GDALGetDriverByName`,
`_base.pyx:106`) and `_io` does its own lookup at `_io.pyx:1429`, and neither
of those registries is ever populated. The result is the symptom that makes
the package useless on iOS:

    DriverRegistrationError: ('No such driver registered: %s', b'GTiff')

raised in the same process that has just listed GTiff among its registered
drivers — because the listing came from `_env`'s registry and the lookup went
to `_base`'s.

Counting call sites rather than definitions makes it unambiguous; every one of
these extensions *defines* the registration symbols, which is why `nm` alone
misleads. `otool -tV`, iOS arm64:

    gdal      _ogr    GDALAllRegister x2, OGRRegisterAll x2, GDALRegister_GTiff x1
    rasterio  _env    GDALAllRegister x1, OGRRegisterAll x1, GDALRegister_GTiff x1
    rasterio  _io     none
    rasterio  _base   none

`osgeo.gdal` escapes the bug for exactly this reason: its `_ogr` registers and
does the work in one extension.

`GDALAllRegister()` is idempotent, so on Android — where a single shared
`libgdal.so` means there was only ever one registry — this changes nothing.

What it does NOT do: the copies stay separate GDAL instances, so configuration
set through `rasterio.Env()` still does not reach `_base` or `_io`. This turns
"cannot open a file at all" into "opens files, with per-extension config". The
complete fix is a shared libgdal for iOS.

